      "question": {
        ":question/id": "56199f2a-cd4b-5efb-ba3a-38fac66558ff",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate the definite integral $\\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{x^{3}}{x^{4} + 9}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x^{4} + 9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =4 x^{3} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =4 x^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u & =x^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^{4} + 9.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 2 \\\\ u & 9 & 25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{(x^{4} + 9)}\\cdot x^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{9}^{25}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{9}^{25}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{9}^{25}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\ln(u)|_{9}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\ln(25)-\\ln(9)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\ln((5)^{2})-\\ln((3)^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2\\ln(5)-2\\ln(3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 2(\\ln(5)-\\ln(3)) \\\\  & =- \\log{\\left(3 \\right)} + \\frac{\\log{\\left(15 \\right)}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
