        ":question/problem": "Calculate the definite integral $\\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{8 x^{3}}{x^{4} + 9}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x^{4} + 9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =4 x^{3} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =4 x^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\,\\text{d}u & =8 x^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^{4} + 9.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 2 \\\\ u & 9 & 25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{(x^{4} + 9)}\\cdot 8 x^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{9}^{25}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{9}^{25}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u & =2\\int_{9}^{25}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\ln(u)|_{9}^{25} \\\\  & =2(\\ln(25)-\\ln(9)) \\\\  & =2(\\ln((5)^{2})-\\ln((3)^{2})) \\\\  & =2(2\\ln(5)-2\\ln(3)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2(\\ln(5)-\\ln(3)) \\\\  & =- 4 \\log{\\left(3 \\right)} + 4 \\log{\\left(5 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
